---
import QualityLayout from "../../../layouts/QualityLayout.astro";
import {
  deliveryMetrics,
  featureTimeline,
} from "../../../data/quality-2026";
---

<QualityLayout
  title="Delivery and Adoption — Counterfact 2026 Quality Audit"
  description="The development pace, test growth, adoption signals, and major product features surrounding Counterfact’s 2026 bug audit."
  canonicalPath="/quality/2026/context"
  active="context"
>
  <header class="quality-page-header">
    <div class="quality-wide">
      <p class="quality-crumbs"><a href="/quality/2026">2026 quality audit</a> / Delivery context</p>
      <p class="quality-kicker">The denominator around the defects</p>
      <h1>More delivery activity, broader tests, more use</h1>
      <p class="quality-deck">
        A low bug count means little without the environment around it. In 2026,
        Counterfact’s delivery rate accelerated, its verification system expanded,
        adoption signals continued upward, and the product’s scope grew materially.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="quality-section quality-wide" aria-labelledby="pace-heading">
    <p class="quality-section-label">Development pace</p>
    <h2 id="pace-heading">Delivery accelerated by several measures</h2>
    <p class="quality-section-intro">
      Same-period comparisons reduce the distortion of comparing a partial year with a full
      one. Pull-request volume includes automation; the note below separates it.
    </p>
    <table class="quality-metric-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Measure</th>
          <th scope="col">2025</th>
          <th scope="col">2026</th>
          <th scope="col">Change</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          deliveryMetrics.map((metric) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                {metric.label}
                <span class="quality-metric-note">{metric.note}</span>
              </td>
              <td>{metric.before}</td>
              <td>{metric.after}</td>
              <td>{metric.change}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="quality-callout">
      Of the 625 merged pull requests in 2026, 314 came from Renovate or Dependabot.
      Removing those dependency bots leaves 311 merges, versus 30 in the same period of
      2025—a 10.4× increase. Of those 311, 197 were authored through GitHub Copilot,
      103 by the maintainer, and 11 by other contributors. Authorship is not a measure of
      review effort or value, but the breakdown shows where the increased volume came from.
    </div>
    <p class="quality-note">
      Pull-request counts are workflow activity, not interchangeable units of product value.
      Release counts can also include patches and dependency-only publications. Both are
      presented as pace indicators, then paired with the feature chronology below.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section">
    <div class="quality-wide quality-split">
      <div class="quality-prose">
        <p class="quality-section-label">Verification</p>
        <h2>Test coverage increased in breadth, depth, and measured percentage</h2>
        <p>
          At the final 2025 release, Counterfact had 35 conventionally named test files and
          240 explicit <code>it()</code>/<code>test()</code> declarations. The September 3
          main branch has 72 files and 904 declarations. Those static counts understate
          parameterized cases, but they provide a consistent repository-level comparison.
        </p>
        <p>
          Coverage also widened structurally. The 2025 Jest configuration collected from one
          <code>src</code> tree; the current configuration covers the client, Counterfact
          facade, generator, OpenAPI, runtime, and REPL packages. Five black-box Gherkin
          journeys now exercise configured portfolios, contract evolution, the developer
          journey, generated OpenAPI, and legacy Swagger behavior through the running product.
        </p>
        <p>
          The measured main-branch line-coverage result rose from 84.26% on 1.4.7 to 91.73%
          on September 3—an increase of 7.47 percentage points. The project’s configured global
          minimums did not rise, so the improvement came from actual covered code rather than
          a relaxed gate. Because the codebase was reorganized into packages, the two
          percentages are informative but not a perfectly fixed-denominator experiment.
        </p>
        <div class="quality-source-links">
          <a href="https://coveralls.io/builds/76781774">1.4.7 coverage result</a>
          <a href="https://coveralls.io/github/counterfact/api-simulator/commit/77f0bc6fd2c3bc935d2eb2da80190369d91f7084">September 3 main coverage</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/counterfact/api-simulator/tree/main/test-black-box/features">Black-box journeys</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="quality-bars" aria-label="Testing growth comparison">
        <div>
          <div class="quality-bar-label"><strong>Test files</strong><span>35 → 72</span></div>
          <div class="quality-bar-row"><span>2025</span><div class="quality-bar-track"><div class="quality-bar-fill" style="width: 48.6%"></div></div></div>
          <div class="quality-bar-row"><span>2026</span><div class="quality-bar-track"><div class="quality-bar-fill" style="width: 100%"></div></div></div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="quality-bar-label"><strong>Test declarations</strong><span>240 → 904</span></div>
          <div class="quality-bar-row"><span>2025</span><div class="quality-bar-track"><div class="quality-bar-fill" style="width: 26.5%"></div></div></div>
          <div class="quality-bar-row"><span>2026</span><div class="quality-bar-track"><div class="quality-bar-fill" style="width: 100%"></div></div></div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="quality-bar-label"><strong>Line coverage</strong><span>84.26% → 91.73%</span></div>
          <div class="quality-bar-row"><span>2025</span><div class="quality-bar-track"><div class="quality-bar-fill" style="width: 84.26%"></div></div></div>
          <div class="quality-bar-row"><span>2026</span><div class="quality-bar-track"><div class="quality-bar-fill" style="width: 91.73%"></div></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section quality-wide" aria-labelledby="usage-heading">
    <p class="quality-section-label">Adoption signals</p>
    <h2 id="usage-heading">Use continued to grow</h2>
    <p class="quality-section-intro">
      Package downloads and repository stars are imperfect: downloads include repeat installs
      and automation, while stars signal interest rather than active use. Together they show
      that Counterfact’s externally visible footprint did not contract during the audit year.
    </p>
    <div class="quality-result-grid">
      <article class="quality-result-card">
        <span class="quality-result-number">62,132</span>
        <span class="quality-result-percent">npm downloads, Jan–Aug 2026</span>
        <span class="quality-result-label">12.2% above the same period in 2025</span>
      </article>
      <article class="quality-result-card" data-tone="feature">
        <span class="quality-result-number">3,222</span>
        <span class="quality-result-percent">npm downloads, Aug 23–29</span>
        <span class="quality-result-label">A date-qualified weekly snapshot</span>
      </article>
      <article class="quality-result-card" data-tone="legacy">
        <span class="quality-result-number">168</span>
        <span class="quality-result-percent">GitHub stars on September 3</span>
        <span class="quality-result-label">Up 28.2% from 131 at year-end 2025</span>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="quality-source-links">
      <a href="https://api.npmjs.org/downloads/point/2025-01-01:2025-08-31/counterfact">2025 npm period</a>
      <a href="https://api.npmjs.org/downloads/point/2026-01-01:2026-08-31/counterfact">2026 npm period</a>
      <a href="https://api.npmjs.org/downloads/point/2026-08-23:2026-08-29/counterfact">Weekly npm snapshot</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/counterfact/api-simulator/stargazers">GitHub stargazers</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section" aria-labelledby="features-heading">
    <div class="quality-wide">
      <p class="quality-section-label">Product scope</p>
      <h2 id="features-heading">Substantial features shipped throughout the year</h2>
      <p class="quality-section-intro">
        The release count reflects more than packaging churn. These milestones changed what
        Counterfact could simulate, how it could be controlled, and how other tools could embed it.
      </p>
      <div class="quality-timeline">
        {
          featureTimeline.map((feature) => (
            <article class="quality-timeline-item">
              <div class="quality-timeline-release">
                {feature.release}
                <span>{feature.date}</span>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h3><a href={feature.href}>{feature.title}</a></h3>
                <p>{feature.detail}</p>
              </div>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="quality-section">
    <div class="quality-reading quality-prose">
      <p class="quality-section-label">Interpretation</p>
      <h2>Why this context changes the quality story</h2>
      <p>
        A defect count by itself is a numerator without a denominator. Counterfact’s public
        development activity nearly tripled, non-dependency delivery activity grew by roughly
        an order of magnitude, releases tripled, and the number and scope of tests expanded.
        Users also had more product surface to exercise and growing reasons to exercise it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Against that backdrop, finding no same-year regression among the ten confirmed 2026
        product defects is useful descriptive evidence. The matching 2025 cohort also contains
        no same-year regression, however, so the comparison does not demonstrate that review,
        tests, agents, or any other workflow input caused the result. The audit cannot see
        unreported bugs, normalize every change by risk, or separate contributors to quality.
      </p>
      <p>
        The defensible claim is narrower: increased velocity and product ambition coincided
        with no observed same-year regression in this defined external-report population. Two
        2026 feature defects and one release-provenance incident prevent that statement from
        becoming a blanket claim that quality “held.”
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</QualityLayout>
